# What does perfect competition assume?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why must every firm be too small to affect the price? How many real markets come close?"
next: {"title":"Why do profits disappear in a competitive market?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/31-why-do-profits-disappear-in-a.cast"}

## One farm, one price
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Q
    y_label: Price
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P
      q_label: ''
      label: ''
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 140
      w: 500
      h: 500
sources:
    - id: fao2023
      title: Cereal Supply and Demand Brief (December 2023)
      authors: FAO
      year: 2023
      finding: World wheat production in 2023 estimated at about 785 million tonnes (forecasts ranged 781–787 Mt during the year).

    text lab_world "World wheat" x 270 y 632 font_size 30 hidden true
    text lab_tonnes "785 million t (2023)" #8f887c cites ["fao2023"] x 270 y 598 font_size 26 hidden true
    arrow f_x #3d3833 from.x 590 from.y 223 to.x 950 to.y 223 hidden true
    arrow f_y #3d3833 from.x 590 from.y 223 to.x 590 to.y 590 hidden true
    text f_xl "her wheat (t)" x 890 y 191 font_size 26 hidden true
    text f_yl "price" x 640 y 585 font_size 26 hidden true
    text lab_farm "One farm" x 770 y 632 font_size 30 hidden true
    group f_axes members ["f_x","f_y","f_xl","f_yl","lab_farm"] hidden true
    path tick #3d3833 points [[745,213],[745,233]] hidden true
    text tick_l "2,000 t" x 745 y 191 font_size 26 hidden true
    text share "1 part in 400,000" #b5482e x 800 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    path bridge dashed #8f887c points [[263,376.5],[590,376.5]] hidden true
    text f_P "P" x 566 y 398.5 font_size 26 hidden true
    dot p10 #b5482e at.x 590 at.y 426.5 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text p10_l "P + 10%" #b5482e x 660 y 426.5 font_size 26 hidden true
    text nothing "nothing sold" #b5482e x 710 y 466.5 font_size 28 hidden true
    path f_dem #2f6b8f points [[590,376.5],[930,376.5]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text f_dem_l "her demand" #2f6b8f x 870 y 406.5 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cue_same "same wheat" #3d3833 x 850 y 545 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cue_known "known prices" #3d3833 x 850 y 508 font_size 28 hidden true
    text taker "price taker" #2f6b8f x 800 y 270 font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "One farm, one price"

What doe (@draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S@)s a perfectly competitive market assume, and does any real market come close? Start with the world's wheat:  (@draw lab_world lab_tonnes@)about 785 million tonnes grown in 2023.

B: An (@draw f_axes@)d one farmer in it.  (@draw tick tick_l@)Say she grows 2,000 tonnes.

B: That's about one part in 400,000 of the world's crop.
    draw share

The world (@draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Pstar@) market sets one price, where supply meets demand. S (@draw bridge f_P@)he sells at that price, P.

Here's the test. She asks (@draw p10 p10_l@) ten percent more than everyone else.

B: I'd guess she loses a few loyal buyers. Maybe a tenth of her sales.
    point at.ref p10 gesture tap

    quiz "One wheat farmer among thousands asks 10% more than the market price. What does she sell?" judge false
        right: Almost nothing. Nobody pays extra for the same wheat.
        * A little less
        * About 10% less
        * Almost nothing

(@draw nothing@) Her wheat is just l (@draw cue_same@)ike everyone else's, and every buyer kn (@draw cue_known@)ows the going price. At ten percent more, they buy from the next farm.

B: Then why not cut her price, to sell more?
    erase p10 p10_l nothing

Because at P she  (@point at.x 745 at.y 376.5 gesture tap@)can already sell every tonne she grows. A cut just gives money away.

So the demand  (@draw f_dem f_dem_l@)she faces is flat: a line at the market price, as far as her output can reach.

B: Even though the world's demand curve slopes down like any other.
    highlight demand_curve

Her tonnes are a drop in that ocean. Whatever she does, the world price doesn't notice.
    highlight share effect underline

    erase share

That's  (@draw taker@)a price taker: she chooses how much to sell, never what to charge.

    pause 0.5

## Why so small?
vars: {"price":{"value":55,"min":20,"max":90}}
use: firm_cost_curves
with: {"mode":"competition","show":["mc","price_line"],"price":55}

    dot big #b5482e at.data [50,36] radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    label big_l "big seller stops here" left #b5482e attach_to big font_size 26 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"rest"}] then rest hidden true
    scratch mathbox work ["profit = P × q − C(q)","one more tonne: profit changes by P − MC","best q: P − MC = 0, so P = MC"] x 500 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch assume work ["Perfect competition:","many small sellers and buyers","identical goods","everyone knows the prices","free entry and exit"] x 500 y 410 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Why so small?"

B: So s (@draw axes@)he can't choose the price. What's left for her to decide is how much to grow.

Every extra (@draw price_line label_price_line@) tonne she sells brings in exactly P. That flat line is h (@highlight price_line@)er demand, and the money from one more tonne.

Growing one (@draw mc label_mc@) more tonne costs more as she pushes her land harder.  (@point at.data [45,25.5] gesture tap@)That rising curve is her marginal cost, MC.

She grows until the last tonne costs ju (@draw q_star p_star label_q_star@)st what it sells for, where P meets MC.

B: Past that point, each tonne costs more than it fetches. So she stops right there.
    point at.data [62,73] gesture tap

    keep price_line q_star

Raise (@animate price 70 duration 4@) the price, and she slides up her MC curve and grows more. Her  (@highlight mc@)MC curve is her supply curve.

B: Now, why must she be small? Suppose she grew half the world's wheat.
    erase price_line_ghost q_star_ghost

Then every e (@draw big big_l@)xtra tonne would push down the price of all the others she sells. Each tonne earns her less than P (@point at.data [50,70] gesture tap@), so she stops short of P equals MC.

B: So that's why every firm must be too small to move the price. Only a price taker grows right up to P equals MC.
    highlight q_star effect circle

    erase big big_l

And P equals MC is the prize: every tonne that's worth more to buyers than it costs to grow gets grown.
    highlight mc

Try it: drag the price up and down, and watch her quantity ride along MC.
    explore params ["price"]

    fade axes mc label_mc price_line label_price_line q_star label_q_star p_star label_p_star to 0.15

B: The math behind P equals MC takes three lines. Open it, or skip it.
    draw mathq

    ask "The math behind P = MC: show it, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

Her profit is the price times her tonnes, minus her total cost.
    draw mathbox_box mathbox_line_1

One more  (@draw mathbox_line_2@)tonne adds P to her revenue and MC to her cost, so profit changes by P minus MC.

Grow while t (@draw mathbox_line_3@)hat's positive, and stop where it hits zero: P equals MC. P stays put only because she's a price taker.

    pause 0.5

    erase mathbox

@rest

    erase mathq

B: Add it all up, and perfect competition as (@draw assume_box assume_line_1 assume_line_2@)sumes four things. Many small sellers and buyers.

B: (@draw assume_line_3@) Identical goods.  (@draw assume_line_4@)Everyone knows the prices. And (@draw assume_line_5@) anyone can enter or leave the market.

    pause 0.5

## Does any market come close?
sources:
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: 'Classroom double-auction markets of about 20–40 traders, each knowing only their own value or cost, converged towards the competitive equilibrium within a few trading periods: ''Even where numbers are "small," there are strong tendencies for a supply and demand competitive equilibrium to be attained.'' Journal of Political Economy 70(2): 111–137.'
    - id: hayek1946
      title: The Meaning of Competition
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1946
      finding: 'Stafford Little Lecture, Princeton, 20 May 1946; in Individualism and Economic Order (1948), pp. 92–106: "''perfect'' competition means indeed the absence of all competitive activities" — advertising, undercutting and improving the product are excluded by definition.'

    cards mkts bins ["Close","Far"] hidden true
        * "Wheat" icon "sheaf of rice" bin Close
        * "Currencies" icon "euro banknote" bin Close
        * "Shares" icon "chart increasing" bin Close
        * "Phones" icon "mobile phone" bin Far
        * "Web search" icon "magnifying glass tilted left" bin Far
        * "Haircuts" icon "barber pole" bin Far
    text h_for "For the model" #2f6b8f x 270 y 620 font_size 38 hidden true
    text h_against "Against" #b5482e x 730 y 620 font_size 38 hidden true
    path divider #8f887c points [[500,330],[500,645]] hidden true
    text pro1 "a benchmark" x 270 y 555 font_size 34 hidden true
    text pro2 "Smith 1962:" cites ["smith1962"] x 270 y 490 font_size 34 hidden true
    text pro2b "prices converge" cites ["smith1962"] x 270 y 450 font_size 34 hidden true
    text con1 "Hayek 1946:" cites ["hayek1946"] x 730 y 555 font_size 34 hidden true
    text con1b "“the absence of all" cites ["hayek1946"] x 730 y 505 font_size 30 hidden true
    text con1c "competitive activities”" cites ["hayek1946"] x 730 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait hayek x 880 y 240 width 190 of "Friedrich Hayek" cameo true hidden true
    text con2 "no rivalry left" #b5482e x 730 y 405 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure1 "Few markets fit every assumption: consensus" #4f7a45 x 500 y 270 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure2 "Still a good guide? contested" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Does any market come close?"

B: Four as (@draw mkts@)sumptions, all at once. So how many real markets pass the test?

    ask "Which markets come close to perfect competition? Sort each one." on mkts store f stage own voice b
        right: Close: wheat, currencies, shares. Far: phones, web search, haircuts.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} right. Close: wheat, currencies, shares. Far: phones, web search, haircuts.

Wheat, currencies and big-company shares come close: one standard product, swarms of traders, and prices on a screen. Most of what you buy sits in the other pile.
    highlight mkts

    erase mkts

B: Then (@draw h_for h_against divider parallel true@) why teach a model that fits so little?

First, as a  (@draw pro1@)benchmark. It tells you exactly what efficiency needs, so you (@highlight pro1 effect underline@) can see which assumption fails, and by how much.

B: And it predicts better than it has any right to: (@draw pro2 pro2b@) Vernon Smith's classroom markets found the competitive price within a few rounds, with only a few dozen traders.

But Friedrich (@draw hayek con1@) Hayek had a sharper objection, in a lecture in 1946.

B: Perfect competition, he sa (@draw con1b con1c@)id, means the absence of all competitive activities. No advertising, no undercutting, no better product.

    erase hayek

Rivalry, the thing we call competing,  (@draw con2@)is a process. The model assumes it has already finished.

B: So how sure are we? That almos (@draw sure1@)t no market meets every assumption: consensus.

Whether the model sti (@draw sure2@)ll guides us where they fail: contested. Smith's markets say often yes; Hayek says it misses what competition is for.

    pause 0.5

    quiz "A city caps taxi licences at 500, and a licence sells for a fortune. Which assumption of perfect competition fails?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: Free entry and exit: nobody new can join, however high the fares, so the cap holds the price up.
        wrong: Can a new driver simply start? Here: free entry and exit fails.
        + Free entry and exit
        * Identical goods
        * Everyone knows the prices

    quiz "Mia's bakery is the only one in Harbourtown that bakes sourdough. Which assumptions fail?" voice b
        right: Many sellers and identical goods: her sourdough has no twin, so she sets her own price.
        wrong: How many sellers of her bread are there, and is it the same as anyone else's? Here: many sellers and identical goods.
        + Many sellers and identical goods
        * Everyone knows the prices
        * None of them

    quiz "One firm makes 40% of all the cement in a region. Why might it produce less than where P = MC?"
        right: Extra cement would lower the price of all it sells, so each extra tonne earns it less than P.
        wrong: What happens to the price when a seller that big adds output? Here: extra cement lowers the price of all it sells.
        + Extra cement would lower the price of all it sells
        * Cement costs a lot to make
        * Its marginal cost is falling

So what does perfect competition assume? Firms too small to move the price, and that is what makes P equal MC. A few markets come close. For the rest, the model is the yardstick we hold them to.
    highlight sure1 sure2

    pause 0.5

## Where next
